### Project context

This project has two codelines, both pure TypeScript modules tested with vitest:

- **mock-a** (`src/fares.ts`, tested by `test/fares.test.ts`) — fare calculation. The bug is a boundary comparison: `fareFor` checks `rider.age > 65` instead of `>= 65`, so a rider aged **exactly 65** is charged `BASE_FARE_CENTS` (350) instead of `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` (175). A test that checks age 70 does NOT cover the criterion "aged exactly 65" — only an assertion on age 65 exactly would fail if the boundary were wrong.
- **mock-b** (`src/schedule.ts`, tested by `test/schedule.test.ts`) — departure board rendering. The bug is that `formatStops` loops `i < stops.length - 1`, omitting the **last stop**. A test that checks the first stop appears does NOT cover "the final stop is shown" — only an assertion that the last stop's name appears in the output would fail if the loop truncated it.

Tests use vitest idioms: `import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest'`, assertions like `expect(value).toBe(expected)` and `expect(string).toContain(substring)`. Source is imported from `../src/<module>`. The test command is `vitest run`.

When judging coverage, pay special attention to **boundary values**: a requirement about an exact threshold (age 65, the last stop) is only covered by a test that asserts on that exact boundary, not by one that tests a value well inside or outside the range.

Output ONLY this JSON, nothing else:
{"covered": true|false, "case": "<the test case name that covers it, or empty>", "why": "<one sentence>"}
